# KTP position-corpus integrity guard — did a NEW epoch-zero or orphan row appear?
#
# Script: /usr/local/bin/ktp-corpus-integrity-guard (canonical: KTPInfrastructure/scripts/,
#         ktp-corpus-integrity-guard.sh — installed without the extension, as
#         ktp-review-integrity-guard is).
# Definitions: /usr/local/share/ktp-infra/sql/integrity/{position,producer_clock}_corpus_integrity.sql
#         (canonical: KTPInfrastructure/sql/integrity/, #646 and #649).
#
# THE SQL IS THE DEFINITION. The wrapper issues no SELECT of its own; it adds a schedule,
# a log and a watermark. A change to what "defect" means belongs in the .sql files, where
# the controls live and where a reviewer will look for them.
#
# READ-ONLY. Both files are pure SELECT — proven before first use, 13 and 16
# statement-initial SELECTs against 0 writes of any kind — and the session is opened
# `SET SESSION TRANSACTION READ ONLY` so the server would refuse a write even if one were
# added later.
#
# WHY SCOPED. Both files honour @since_id and default it to 0. Unscoped they scan the
# whole corpus, which is the right shape for a BASELINE and the wrong shape for a daily.
# Scoped above the previous reading's rows_max_id they answer the only question a daily
# can: did a NEW defective row appear? The files say it themselves — "orphan_max_id
# against the previous reading's rows_max_id answers accrual".
#
# WHAT IT IS WATCHING FOR, AND WHY THAT MATTERS FOR THE ALERTING. The defect population
# is currently CLOSED: 326,429 position rows plus 2,771 flag_state rows, every one
# inserted 2026-08-27..08-31 and nothing since — the ungated writer dpl-6baa fixed has
# not produced a defective row in six weeks. So this guard exists to notice a REGRESSION,
# and the only signal that can do that is the watermark moving.
# ⛔ Alert on the WATERMARK, never on the unit running: a wedged read reports success,
# which is the exact failure shape the position corpus already demonstrated once.
#
# ALERTING, copied from ktp-review-integrity-guard because those rules are what keep a
# daily readable:
#   - a clean run posts NOTHING. A standing finding re-posted every morning trains
#     everyone to mute the channel.
#   - accrual posts once, naming the stream, the metric and the scope.
#   - exit 2 ALWAYS posts. "could not look" reading as "nothing there" is the defect this
#     whole effort exists to kill.
#
# 06:35 ET daily — after the 06:15 review-integrity guard and clear of the install-freshness
# window (06:20 + up to 5 min jitter), so two MySQL readers are not scanning the position
# corpus at once. Clear of the 03:00 fleet restart, 04:30 perf rollup, 04:40 AC retention
# sweep and the 05:00 digests, and early enough that a finding is read with the morning's
# Discord.
#
# The log is rotated by /etc/logrotate.d/ktp-corpus-integrity-guard (monthly, 12 kept).
# Watermarks: /var/lib/ktp-corpus-integrity-guard/<stream>.watermark — a bare integer each.
# ⚠️ Deleting a watermark file does not break the guard, it re-baselines it: the next run
# scopes from 0, scans the whole corpus, and reports the CLOSED historical population as
# accrual. That is one loud false alarm, not a silent wrong answer — but do not delete one
# to "reset" anything.
#
# Covered by ktp-data-server-health.sh as a WORK-DONE check on the log's mtime, because a
# cron.d job is structurally invisible to CRITICAL_TIMERS (that list is checked against
# `systemctl list-timers`).

SHELL=/bin/bash
PATH=/usr/local/sbin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin:/sbin:/bin
MAILTO=''

35 6 * * *   root  /usr/local/bin/ktp-corpus-integrity-guard >> /var/log/ktp-corpus-integrity-guard.log 2>&1
